WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:02.034
-Our next guest is an author

00:00:02.034 --> 00:00:03.536
and staff writer
at "The Atlantic."

00:00:03.536 --> 00:00:05.738
His new book,
"Romney: A Reckoning,"
is on sale now.

00:00:05.738 --> 00:00:08.875
Please welcome back to the show
McKay Coppins, everybody.

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:10.610
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:21.354 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:23.456
Welcome back to the show.
-Thanks for having me.

00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:26.225
-So, this is
a very impressive book

00:00:26.225 --> 00:00:30.262
because of the amount of access
you were granted by Mitt Romney.

00:00:30.262 --> 00:00:32.598
He gave you journals,
he gave you texts,

00:00:32.598 --> 00:00:36.135
he gave you access
to e-mails he wrote.

00:00:36.135 --> 00:00:39.372
Are you surprised
at how much he gave you?

00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:42.308
-I was surprised when
he agreed to do it at all.

00:00:42.308 --> 00:00:45.211
-Yeah.
-Because, you know, Mitt Romney,

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:47.446
was known for most
of his political career

00:00:47.446 --> 00:00:50.149
as, like, this very cautious,

00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:52.819
you know, restrained,
calculated politician

00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:54.620
who was stuck
to his talking points.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:56.790
And when I approached him
about doing this book,

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.458
I told him,
"I only want to do this

00:00:58.458 --> 00:01:00.926
if you're ready
to be fully candid."

00:01:00.926 --> 00:01:03.729
And I knew he was on board

00:01:03.729 --> 00:01:05.264
when like a couple weeks
into it,

00:01:05.264 --> 00:01:06.499
he sent me a text.

00:01:06.499 --> 00:01:09.168
I was actually at church.
I got a text from him.

00:01:09.168 --> 00:01:10.703
He says,
"Hey. Check your e-mail.

00:01:10.703 --> 00:01:12.505
I sent you something
that might be interesting."

00:01:12.505 --> 00:01:15.274
And it was just hundreds
of pages of his journals.

00:01:15.274 --> 00:01:16.709
And I was like, "Oh, wow.

00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:18.544
Alright.
We're gonna do this for real."

00:01:18.544 --> 00:01:20.413
-He already was --

00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:23.316
He sort of started
as an outsider in the Senate.

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:27.153
You know, he had been vocally
opposed to Donald Trump,

00:01:27.153 --> 00:01:28.888
especially on character.

00:01:28.888 --> 00:01:30.923
And then he joins the Senate,

00:01:30.923 --> 00:01:33.659
where the GOP caucus
is obviously very full-throated

00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:35.161
in their defense
of Donald Trump.

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:37.530
Yet, I imagine he has to be
even more on the outside

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.265
now that this book has come out.

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:41.367
Have you spoken to him
since it's been released?

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:43.302
-Yeah, well, a few weeks ago,

00:01:43.302 --> 00:01:45.838
like, the first excerpts
from the book came out,

00:01:45.838 --> 00:01:49.508
and, you know, he says
some very damning things

00:01:49.508 --> 00:01:52.278
about a lot of his colleagues
in the Senate.

00:01:52.278 --> 00:01:55.114
And I checked in with his staff.

00:01:55.114 --> 00:01:56.949
I was like,
"How's everything going?"

00:01:56.949 --> 00:01:59.185
They were like, "He's skipping
the caucus lunches

00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:01.187
for a little while.
-Yeah.

00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:02.655
-He's doing okay."

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:04.657
I mean, he knew that
this was gonna make for,

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:07.860
like, a weird few weeks
or months at work.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:11.030
But it's to his credit
that he went along with it.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.166
-Well, you know,
it's called "A Reckoning,"

00:02:13.166 --> 00:02:15.468
and that's a very apt title
for this book.

00:02:15.468 --> 00:02:18.738
And even you talk about how he
was very careful with his words.

00:02:18.738 --> 00:02:22.008
It does feel like this moment

00:02:22.008 --> 00:02:25.411
has broken him to some degree
and it's broken his spirit.

00:02:25.411 --> 00:02:29.014
And he is using this
as a warning to all of us.

00:02:29.014 --> 00:02:32.684
-I think that's why he
ultimately decided to do this.

00:02:32.684 --> 00:02:36.355
I approached him just
a few weeks after January 6th,

00:02:36.355 --> 00:02:39.359
and I still remember
our first meeting

00:02:39.359 --> 00:02:42.127
was in his, like,
little Senate office.

00:02:42.127 --> 00:02:44.531
The barbed-wire fence
was still around the Capitol.

00:02:44.531 --> 00:02:47.666
And he --
Like, something snapped in him

00:02:47.666 --> 00:02:49.768
almost after January 6th

00:02:49.768 --> 00:02:54.907
because it demonstrated
just how far his party

00:02:54.907 --> 00:02:58.244
had kind of descended into what
he considers authoritarianism.

00:02:58.244 --> 00:03:00.279
He told me in one
of our first meetings,

00:03:00.279 --> 00:03:02.948
you know, "A very large portion
of my party

00:03:02.948 --> 00:03:05.184
really doesn't believe
in the Constitution."

00:03:05.184 --> 00:03:07.853
And I think that was
eye-opening for him.

00:03:07.853 --> 00:03:12.791
And he decided that it was time
to sort of tell the stories

00:03:12.791 --> 00:03:16.129
of what he had seen
inside these caucus rooms,

00:03:16.129 --> 00:03:18.864
inside Republican politics
over the last 30 years

00:03:18.864 --> 00:03:21.367
so that Americans would know,

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:22.901
you know,
just what we're up against,

00:03:22.901 --> 00:03:25.504
what's happening and what's
happening to his party.

00:03:25.504 --> 00:03:28.307
-There's a very real, obviously,
threat he's addressing.

00:03:28.307 --> 00:03:30.209
There's also, you know,
very funny ways

00:03:30.209 --> 00:03:32.344
he describes his colleagues.

00:03:32.344 --> 00:03:34.480
Well, not that funny.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:38.384
Ted Cruz. "Scary,
demagogue, frightening."

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:40.719
Mitch McConnell told Romney,
"You're lucky you can say

00:03:40.719 --> 00:03:42.188
the things we all think."

00:03:42.188 --> 00:03:43.722
That is one of
Romney's messages,

00:03:43.722 --> 00:03:45.892
is he isn't actually
the only one who thinks this.

00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:48.027
He's the only one
who says it out loud.

00:03:48.027 --> 00:03:50.329
J.D. Vance. "I don't know
that I can disrespect

00:03:50.329 --> 00:03:53.866
someone more than J.D. Vance.

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:57.203
How do you sit
next to him at lunch?"

00:03:57.203 --> 00:04:00.540
-And now he's stuck
sitting next to him at lunch.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.274
-Yeah.
-I mean, you know, look.

00:04:02.274 --> 00:04:05.978
Like, when these quotes
started to come out,

00:04:05.978 --> 00:04:08.247
people were like, "Oh,
Mitt Romney is being petty,"

00:04:08.247 --> 00:04:10.149
or he's, you know,
consumed with these

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:11.417
old resentments or whatever.

00:04:11.417 --> 00:04:13.352
But, like, I think it's really

00:04:13.352 --> 00:04:14.821
he's kind of heartbroken.

00:04:14.821 --> 00:04:16.922
Like, he believed in this party.

00:04:16.922 --> 00:04:19.525
He believed in what he thought

00:04:19.525 --> 00:04:21.560
were the core tenets
of the Republican Party.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.329
And instead
what it turns out is,

00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:25.898
like, all these guys
that he once respected

00:04:25.898 --> 00:04:27.432
just lined up behind

00:04:27.432 --> 00:04:30.803
this, like, manifestly crazy
person in Donald Trump.

00:04:30.803 --> 00:04:32.204
And now he, you know --

00:04:32.204 --> 00:04:33.973
And they're all telling him
in private,

00:04:33.973 --> 00:04:36.842
"We agree with you, Mitt, but
we can't say the same things

00:04:36.842 --> 00:04:38.478
because we have to
win re-election."

00:04:38.478 --> 00:04:42.448
And so I think that, you know,
he's well within his rights

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:45.418
to say a few of these quotes
that we have in the book.

00:04:45.418 --> 00:04:47.687
-And it's also interesting
because this is --

00:04:47.687 --> 00:04:50.056
You know, you sort of have
documentary evidence.

00:04:50.056 --> 00:04:51.624
This is not something
that he's doing now.

00:04:51.624 --> 00:04:53.425
These are things that
he has written in journals.

00:04:53.425 --> 00:04:55.461
These are things --
These are thoughts he's had...

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:58.964
-Going back a decade or longer.
Yeah.

00:04:58.964 --> 00:05:01.133
-Donald Trump --
it won't surprise anybody --

00:05:01.133 --> 00:05:03.336
was not a fan of the book.

00:05:03.336 --> 00:05:05.371
But I should note this is --
I want to just read

00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:09.742
the first couple lines
because this is a biography.

00:05:09.742 --> 00:05:11.510
-Correct.
-You wrote this book.

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:13.112
-That's right.

00:05:13.112 --> 00:05:15.481
-"Mitt Romney, a total loser
that only a mother could love,

00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:19.152
just wrote a book
which is much like him --

00:05:19.152 --> 00:05:21.053
boring, horrible,
and totally predictable."

00:05:21.053 --> 00:05:25.858
So...Donald Trump thinks
that Mitt Romney wrote a book.

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:27.493
-He did. [ Laughs ]

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:30.629
He seems slightly confused
between biography and memoir.

00:05:30.629 --> 00:05:31.964
-Yeah.
-In his defense,

00:05:31.964 --> 00:05:35.267
he probably has never read
one of either, so...

00:05:35.267 --> 00:05:36.935
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:05:36.935 --> 00:05:38.137
-I think that's accurate.

00:05:38.137 --> 00:05:42.975
-I will say --
I will say I texted it --

00:05:42.975 --> 00:05:45.511
When I saw that,
I texted that to Mitt.

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:48.714
And his response --
his response was,

00:05:48.714 --> 00:05:51.884
"Ha ha ha.
He's such a whack job."

00:05:51.884 --> 00:05:53.385
That's right.

00:05:53.385 --> 00:05:56.722
-It's so funny because, like,
Trump might think he wrote it

00:05:56.722 --> 00:05:58.291
because his face
is on the cover,

00:05:58.291 --> 00:06:02.395
but if that's true,
Trump wrote 100 books this year.

00:06:02.395 --> 00:06:06.866
Like, Donald Trump's on
the cover of every other book.

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:09.435
You also -- It does seem like --

00:06:09.435 --> 00:06:11.003
Certainly as excerpts
coming out,

00:06:11.003 --> 00:06:13.039
a lot of the press
is talking about the moment

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:15.274
we're living in now,
but in 2012,

00:06:15.274 --> 00:06:18.744
Romney obviously was the GOP
nominee running against Obama.

00:06:18.744 --> 00:06:20.913
And you discovered --
Not a thing you knew.

00:06:20.913 --> 00:06:23.682
Not a thing people are paying
that much attention to now.

00:06:23.682 --> 00:06:25.784
That, in 2012,
six weeks before the election,

00:06:25.784 --> 00:06:27.820
he almost dropped out.
-Yeah. This is one of

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:30.056
the most fascinating things
I found in his journals.

00:06:30.056 --> 00:06:32.825
Like, you know, it was after

00:06:32.825 --> 00:06:35.694
that hot-mic moment came out.

00:06:35.694 --> 00:06:38.597
Remember when he talked
about the 47% of Americans

00:06:38.597 --> 00:06:41.768
who would never vote for him
and he kind of disparaged them?

00:06:41.768 --> 00:06:43.969
It was in a closed-door
fundraiser.

00:06:43.969 --> 00:06:47.406
So, that tape came out,
and, you know, at the time,

00:06:47.406 --> 00:06:49.375
publicly,
he tried to defend himself

00:06:49.375 --> 00:06:51.978
and say the comments
are being taken out of context

00:06:51.978 --> 00:06:53.345
or whatever.

00:06:53.345 --> 00:06:57.082
In his journals, he was
anguished over this coming out

00:06:57.082 --> 00:07:00.219
to the point where people
in his circle thought he was,

00:07:00.219 --> 00:07:01.653
like, clinically depressed.

00:07:01.653 --> 00:07:03.789
He couldn't sleep.
he couldn't eat.

00:07:03.789 --> 00:07:05.624
He would every night
go into his journals

00:07:05.624 --> 00:07:08.594
and write like, "I'm so stupid.
I can't believe I did this."

00:07:08.594 --> 00:07:10.463
Really beating himself up.

00:07:10.463 --> 00:07:12.464
And he writes about
in his journals

00:07:12.464 --> 00:07:15.133
one night, six weeks
before the election,

00:07:15.133 --> 00:07:17.236
he called the chief strategist
of his campaign

00:07:17.236 --> 00:07:19.938
and said,
"I think I need to drop out.

00:07:19.938 --> 00:07:23.409
You should let Chris Christie
or somebody take take the job."

00:07:23.409 --> 00:07:26.145
That's kind of
how depressed he was.

00:07:26.145 --> 00:07:27.746
I think it also speaks to, like,

00:07:27.746 --> 00:07:29.715
there's something about
Mitt Romney where he, like,

00:07:29.715 --> 00:07:31.951
desperately hates
letting people down.

00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:34.720
And he felt like with that gaffe

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:36.822
that was kind of
derailing his campaign,

00:07:36.822 --> 00:07:39.858
he had let down his, you know,
the people who supported him,

00:07:39.858 --> 00:07:41.827
the people
who had donated to him.

00:07:41.827 --> 00:07:44.062
You know, he eventually
pulled himself back up,

00:07:44.062 --> 00:07:45.931
and he lost the election,

00:07:45.931 --> 00:07:49.168
but, you know, that moment,
I thought, was really revealing.

00:07:49.168 --> 00:07:50.803
-It must be a weird time for him

00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:52.671
because he is still
a conservative.

00:07:52.671 --> 00:07:54.740
He has conservative principles.

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:58.677
He stands for a lot
of conservative ideals,

00:07:58.677 --> 00:08:02.714
and yet his party
has, you know, ostracized him.

00:08:02.714 --> 00:08:05.651
There are times where the left,
you know, eats it up

00:08:05.651 --> 00:08:07.720
when he says negative things
about his colleagues,

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:12.124
but ultimately, he's no hero
of the left, either.

00:08:12.124 --> 00:08:16.395
One thing that I did find
endearing on a human level

00:08:16.395 --> 00:08:19.198
is he has connected
with President Biden

00:08:19.198 --> 00:08:22.000
on just the idea
of what it's like to age.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:25.204
-Yes, he has struck up
this friendship with Joe Biden

00:08:25.204 --> 00:08:27.506
the last couple of years
because he found that, like,

00:08:27.506 --> 00:08:30.609
they actually get along,
you know, on some things

00:08:30.609 --> 00:08:32.744
even though
they disagree on politics.

00:08:32.744 --> 00:08:34.646
And they spend
a lot of their time

00:08:34.646 --> 00:08:37.649
commiserating about
the indignities of getting old

00:08:37.649 --> 00:08:40.052
as two old guys are wont to do.

00:08:40.052 --> 00:08:44.189
But there's this one story
about, apparently,

00:08:44.189 --> 00:08:46.491
during one of these calls,
he told Joe Biden,

00:08:46.491 --> 00:08:49.027
"You know, I've been watching
some of your public events

00:08:49.027 --> 00:08:52.532
and you shuffle when you walk
and it makes you look old.

00:08:52.532 --> 00:08:55.067
And one of the tricks
that I've learned

00:08:55.067 --> 00:08:56.669
is take longer strides.

00:08:56.669 --> 00:08:59.138
It'll make you look youthful
and energetic."

00:08:59.138 --> 00:09:02.007
And so, apparently,
a few weeks later,

00:09:02.007 --> 00:09:03.475
Joe Biden called him back
and said,

00:09:03.475 --> 00:09:06.145
"Mitt, I took your advice
and it worked."

00:09:06.145 --> 00:09:08.080
[ Laughter ]

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:10.382
He said, "I had given this --
I gave a speech,

00:09:10.382 --> 00:09:12.585
and I finished,
and I was leaving the stage,

00:09:12.585 --> 00:09:14.887
and I made a point
to take big, long steps.

00:09:14.887 --> 00:09:17.256
And I got off,
and the first lady was there,

00:09:17.256 --> 00:09:19.625
and she said,
'What's gotten into you?!'"

00:09:19.625 --> 00:09:22.127
[ Laughter ]

00:09:22.127 --> 00:09:24.029
-Well, he should write a book,

00:09:24.029 --> 00:09:26.965
like, "How to Walk
Impressively."

00:09:26.965 --> 00:09:29.034
Yeah, exactly.
Congratulations on this.

00:09:29.034 --> 00:09:31.036
Thanks so much
for being here tonight.

00:09:31.036 --> 00:09:33.472
I really enjoy talking to you.
McKay Coppins, everybody.

00:09:33.472 --> 00:09:35.040
"Romney: A Reckoning"
is out now.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:36.876
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

